.TH MXPLORER 1 "2026-11-28" "mxplorer @MXPLORER_VERSION_STRING@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mxplorer \- terminal file manager built on a UI independent core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mxplorer
.RI [ command " [" argument " ...]]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mxplorer
is a small file manager for Linux.
Started without arguments it prints a prompt and executes commands until you
leave it, so the current directory is kept between commands.
Started with a command it executes that one command, prints the result and
exits with the status of that command, which makes it usable from scripts.
.PP
Paths may be absolute, relative, or start with
.BR ~ " for the home directory."
.B ~user
is looked up in the passwd database.
The special path
.B -
refers to the previous directory.
Arguments may be quoted with single or double quotes and backslash escapes
are honoured outside single quotes.
.PP
In interactive mode, Tab completes command names, filesystem paths and short
or long options.  Path completion expands
.B ~
and
.BR ~user ,
keeps directory separators and escapes spaces.  A directory candidate ends
with
.BR / ;
pressing Tab again lists alternatives when the common prefix cannot be
extended.  The line editor supports the left/right arrow keys, Home, End,
Backspace, Delete, and the usual Ctrl-A/E/B/F/U/K/W/L editing keys.
.PP
Column output, cursor movement and completion wrapping use terminal cell width
rather than UTF-8 byte length, so CJK and fullwidth names align correctly under
a UTF-8 locale.
.PP
Every short option has an exact double-dash long form.  Boolean examples are
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-\-long ,
.BR \-\-recursive ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-preserve
and
.BR \-\-parents .
Options that take a value accept both
.B \-\-name value
and
.BR \-\-name=value ;
for example,
.B tree \-\-level 2
and
.BR "find --depth=1" .
A standalone
.B \-\-
stops option parsing, so paths whose names begin with a dash remain usable.
.B command \-\-help
shows the command-specific usage and option list.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI ls " [\-a] [\-l] [path]"
List the entries of a directory.
Directories are marked with a trailing
.BR / ,
symbolic links with a trailing
.BR @ .
.TP
.B ll
Shorthand for
.BR "ls \-l" .
.TP
.BI cd " [path]"
Change the current directory.
Without an argument it goes to the home directory.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the current directory.
.TP
.BI cp " [\-r] [\-f] [\-p] " source " ... " destination
Copy files and directories.
When several sources or an existing directory as destination are given the
sources are copied into the destination, like
.BR cp (1).
Symbolic links are recreated rather than followed.
Copying writes a temporary file and renames it into place, so an interrupted
copy never leaves a partial destination behind.
For a directory target that does not exist yet, the whole tree is first built
under a hidden sibling staging directory and renamed into place only after it
succeeds.  Merging into an already existing directory is not transactional.
With
.B \-p
permission bits (setuid, setgid and the sticky bit included), timestamps and
extended attributes are preserved, ACLs travel as the posix_acl attributes,
and hard links between the copied files stay hard links.
Sparse files stay sparse.
.TP
.BI mv " [\-f] [\-p] " source " ... " destination
Move or rename files and directories.
Within one file system this uses
.BR rename (2);
across file systems it falls back to copy and delete.
.TP
.BI rm " [\-r] [\-f] " path " ..."
Remove files, or whole trees with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI mkdir " [\-p] " path " ..."
Create directories.
With
.B \-p
missing parents are created and existing directories are not an error.
.TP
.BI stat " path " ..."
Show type, size, modification time and permission bits.
.TP
.BI tree " [path] [\-L " depth "]"
Draw the directory hierarchy, limited to
.I depth
levels if given.
.TP
.BI find " " pattern " [" path " ...] [" options "]"
Search for entries whose name matches
.IR pattern .
The pattern is matched against the name, not the whole path.
Without
.B \-E
or
.B \-F
it is a glob
.RB ( \(aq*\(aq ,
.RB \(aq?\(aq
and
.RB \(aq[...]\(aq );
a glob that contains no wildcard is treated as
.BR *pattern* ,
so
.B report
also finds
.BR quarterly-report.pdf .
Several starting paths may be given; every path is checked before anything is
printed.
Matching paths are printed to standard output, one absolute path per line; the
summary and any warnings go to standard error, so the output can be piped.
.TP
.B search
Alias of
.BR find .
.TP
.B help
List the available commands.
.TP
.B clear
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B exit
Leave the shell.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Include hidden entries (names starting with a dot).
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-long
Long listing: permissions, size, modification time.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Recurse into directories.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Overwrite existing destinations, and ignore paths that do not exist when
removing.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-preserve
Keep permissions, timestamps, extended attributes (including ACLs) and hard
links between the copied files.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-parents
Create missing parent directories.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-ignore-case
Ignore case while searching.
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-regex
Treat the pattern as an ECMAScript regular expression.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-literal
Treat the pattern as plain text, matched as a substring.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-type " f|d|l"
Only regular files, only directories, or only symbolic links.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-depth " depth"
Do not descend deeper than
.I depth
levels; 1 means the direct children of the starting point.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-limit " count"
Stop after
.I count
matches.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-content " text"
Only match regular files whose contents contain
.IR text .
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-size " +N[KMG]"
Only match entries larger than
.I N
bytes;
.B \-\-size \-N[KMG]
matches entries smaller than
.IR N .
The unit letters are 1024-based.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-modified " +N[dhms]"
Only match entries modified longer ago than
.IR N ;
.B \-\-modified \-N[dhms]
matches entries modified more recently than
.IR N .
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-level " depth"
Limit the
.B tree
command to
.I depth
levels below the starting point.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the command list.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List the current directory, hidden entries included:
.B mxplorer ls -a
.TP
Copy a tree and keep permission bits:
.B mxplorer cp -rp src backup/
.TP
Show two levels of the hierarchy below the home directory:
.B mxplorer tree ~ -L 2
.TP
Find PDFs anywhere below the home directory, longest list limited to 20:
.B mxplorer find '*.pdf' ~ -n 20
.TP
Find files whose contents mention a word, older than a week:
.B mxplorer find '*' ~/Documents -c needle -m +7d
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on failure, which includes a cancelled operation.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ls (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR tree (1)
